# Local Assistant prompts sound like the user

Whenever you send a message to the in-editor local AI Assistant (Ollama / Assistant window), the prompt must sound exactly like the user: a non-coder speaking plain English.

## Required

- Write like everyday conversation — short, direct, goal-focused.
- Describe what you want to happen in the game or editor, not how to implement it.
- Keep Cursor’s own audits, greps, and code checks out of the Assistant prompt. Verify privately; prompt conversationally.

## Forbidden in Assistant prompts

- API names, class/service names, file paths, ids (`part-8`, etc.)
- Code, pseudocode, math/vector recipes, Enums, signal/event names
- Implementation instructions (“flatten Y”, “LookVector”, “connect InputBegan”, “AssemblyLinearVelocity”, …)
- Anything a person with no coding knowledge would not say

## Examples

```
❌ BAD
That still is not camera-relative. You used the Player part Orientation yaw.
Fix the movement Script so W/A/S/D use workspace.CurrentCamera.CFrame
look/right vectors flattened on Y (set Y to 0 then Unit).

✅ GOOD
WASD still feels wrong — I want to move based on where the camera is looking,
not the way the player part is facing. Please fix that. Keep jump working,
and don’t leave extra movement scripts on the Player.
```

```
❌ BAD
Use UIS:IsKeyDown(Enum.KeyCode.Space) with a grounded cooldown; do not call
InputBegan inside Heartbeat.

✅ GOOD
Jump still doesn’t work when I press Space. Can you fix jumping without
breaking the camera-based movement?
```

If a prior Assistant result was wrong, say what still feels wrong in plain language and ask them to fix it — do not dictate the technical solution in the prompt.
